// `@compileFor` on a member is a HARD ERROR rather than a no-op, and that is the whole point:
// pruneInactiveDecls walks TOP-LEVEL declarations only, so a member's gate is never evaluated and never
// stripped. Accepting it would leave a conditional that silently fails open on every build — the same
// shape as the `@compileFor(NATIVE)` spelling that silently deleted code in a loose build.
// Gating the enclosing `type` already drops every member with it.
type value Probe {
    public int32 n;
    public ctor make(int32 n) { this.n = n; }
    @compileFor(DEBUG) public fn int32 trace() { return 251; }
}
fn int32 main() { return 0; }
